\"right\", \"left\"] | None = None,\n8731 |         label: Literal[\"right\", \"left\"] | None = None,\n8732 |         convention: Literal[\"start\", \"end\", \"s\", \"e\"] | lib.NoDefault = lib.no_default,\n8733 |         on: Level | None = None,\n8734 |         level: Level | None = None,\n8735 |         origin: str | TimestampConvertibleTypes = \"start_day\",\n8736 |         offset: TimedeltaConvertibleTypes | None = None,\n8737 |         group_keys: bool = False,\n8738 |     ) -> Resampler:\n8739 |         \"\"\"\n8740 |         Resample time-series data.\n8741 | \n8742 |         Convenience method for frequency conversion and resampling of time series.\n8743 |         The object must have a datetime-like index (`DatetimeIndex`, `PeriodIndex`,\n8744 |         or `TimedeltaIndex`), or the caller must pass the label of a datetime-like\n8745 |         series/index to the ``on``/``level`` keyword parameter.\n8746 | \n8747 |         Parameters\n8748 |         ----------\n8749 |         rule : DateOffset, Timedelta or str\n8750 |             The offset string or object representing target conversion.\n8751 |         closed : {{'right', 'left'}}, default None\n8752 |             Which side of bin interval is closed. The default is 'left'\n8753 |             for all frequency offsets except for 'ME', 'YE', 'QE', 'BME',\n8754 |             'BA', 'BQE', and 'W' which all have a default of 'right'.\n8755 |         label : {{'right', 'left'}}, default None\n8756 |             Which bin edge label to label bucket with. The default is 'left'\n8757 |             for all frequency offsets except for 'ME', 'YE', 'QE', 'BME',\n8758 |             'BA', 'BQE', and 'W' which all have a default of 'right'.\n8759 |         convention : {{'start', 'end', 's', 'e'}}, default 'start'\n8760 |             For `PeriodIndex` only, controls whether to use the start or\n8761 |             end of `rule`.\n8762 | \n8763 |             .. deprecated:: 2.2.0\n8764 |                 Convert PeriodIndex to DatetimeIndex before resampling instead.\n8765 |         on : str, optional\n8766 |             For a DataFrame, column to use instead of index for resampling.\n8767 |             Column must be datetime-like.\n8768 |         level : str or int, optional\n8769 |             For a MultiIndex, level (name or number) to use for\n8770 |             resampling. `level` must be datetime-like.\n8771 |         origin : Timestamp or str, default 'start_day'\n8772 |             The timestamp on which to adjust the grouping. The timezone of origin\n8773 |             must match the timezone of the index.\n8774 |             If string, must be Timestamp convertible or one of the following:\n8775 | \n8776 |             - 'epoch': `origin` is 1970-01-01\n8777 |             - 'start': `origin` is the first value of the timeseries\n8778 |             - 'start_day': `origin` is the first day at midnight of the timeseries\n8779 | \n8780 |             - 'end': `origin` is the last value of the timeseries\n8781 |             - 'end_day': `origin` is the ceiling midnight of the last day\n8782 | \n8783 |             .. versionadded:: 1.3.0\n8784 | \n8785 |             .. note::\n8786 | \n8787 |                 Only takes effect for Tick-frequencies (i.e. fixed frequencies like\n8788 |                 days, hours, and minutes, rather than months or quarters).\n8789 |         offset : Timedelta or str, default is None\n8790 |             An offset timedelta added to the origin.\n8791 | \n8792 |         group_keys : bool, default False\n8793 |             Whether to include the group keys in the result index when using\n8794 |             ``.apply()`` on the resampled object.\n8795 | \n8796 |             .. versionadded:: 1.5.0\n8797 | \n8798 |                 Not specifying ``group_keys`` will retain values-dependent behavior\n8799 |                 from pandas 1.4 and earlier (see :ref:`pandas 1.5.0 Release notes\n8800 |                 <whatsnew_150.enhancements.resample_group_keys>` for examples).\n8801 | \n8802 |             .. versionchanged:: 2.0.0\n8803 | \n8804 |                 ``group_keys`` now defaults to ``False``.\n8805 | \n8806 |         Returns\n8807 |         -------\n8808 |         pandas.api.typing.Resampler\n8809 |             :class:`~pandas.core.Resampler` object.\n8810 | \n8811 |         See Also\n8812 |         --------\n8813 |         Series.resample : Resample a Series.\n8814 |         DataFrame.resample : Resample a DataFrame.\n8815 |         groupby : Group {klass} by mapping, function, label, or list of labels.\n8816 |         asfreq : Reindex a {klass} with the given frequency without grouping.\n8817 | \n8818 |         Notes\n8819 |         -----\n8820 |         See the `user guide\n8821 |         <https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/user_guide/timeseries.html#resampling>`__\n8822 |         for more.\n8823 | \n8824 |         To learn more about the offset strings, please see `this link\n8825 |         <https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/user_guide/timeseries.html#dateoffset-objects>`__.\n8826 | \n8827 |         Examples\n8828 |         --------\n8829 |         Start by creating a series with 9 one minute timestamps.\n8830 | \n8831 |         >>> index = pd.date_range(\"1/1/2000\", periods=9, freq=\"min\")\n8832 |         >>> series = pd.Series(range(9), index=index)\n8833 |         >>> series\n8834 |         2000-01-01 00:00:00    0\n8835 |         2000-01-01 00:01:00    1\n8836 |         2000-01-01 00:02:00    2\n8837 |         2000-01-01 00:03:00    3\n8838 |         2000-01-01 00:04:00    4\n8839 |         2000-01-01 00:05:00    5\n8840 |         2000-01-01 00:06:00    6\n8841 |         2000-01-01 00:07:00    7\n8842 |         2000-01-01 00:08:00    8\n8843 |         Freq: min, dtype: int64\n8844 | \n8845 |         Downsample the series into 3 minute bins and sum the values\n8846 |         of the timestamps falling into a bin.\n8847 | \n8848 |         >>> series.resample(\"3min\").sum()\n8849 |         2000-01-01 00:00:00     3\n8850 |         2000-01-01 00:03:00    12\n8851 |         2000-01-01 00:06:00    21\n8852 |         Freq: 3min, dtype: int64\n8853 | \n8854 |         Downsample the series into 3 minute bins as above, but label each\n8855 |         bin using the right edge instead of the left. Please note that the\n8856 |         value in the bucket used as the label is not included in the bucket,\n8857 |         which it labels. For example, in the original series the\n8858 |         bucket ``2000-01-01 00:03:00`` contains the value 3, but the summed\n8859 |         value in the resampled bucket with the label ``2000-01-01 00:03:00``\n8860 |         does not include 3 (if it did, the summed value would be 6, not 3).\n8861 | \n8862 |         >>> series.resample(\"3min\", label=\"right\").sum()\n8863 |         2000-01-01 00:03:00     3\n8864 |         2000-01-01 00:06:00    12\n8865 |         2000-01-01 00:09:00    21\n8866 |         Freq: 3min, dtype: int64\n8867 | \n8868 |         To include this value close the right side of the bin interval,\n8869 |         as shown below.\n8870 | \n8871 |         >>> series.resample(\"3min\", label=\"right\", closed=\"right\").sum()\n8872 |         2000-01-01 00:00:00     0\n8873 |         2000-01-01 00:03:00     6\n8874 |         2000-01-01 00:06:00    15\n8875 |         2000-01-01 00:09:00    15\n8876 |         Freq: 3min, dtype: int64\n8877 | \n8878 |         Upsample the series into 30 second bins.\n8879 | \n8880 |         >>> series.resample(\"30s\").asfreq()[0:5]  # Select first 5 rows\n8881 |         2000-01-01 00:00:00   0.0\n8882 |         2000-01-01 00:00:30   NaN\n8883 |         2000-01-01 00:01:00   1.0\n8884 |         2000-01-01 00:01:30   NaN\n8885 |         2000-01-01 00:02:00   2.0\n8886 |         Freq: 30s, dtype: float64\n8887 | \n8888 |         Upsample the series into 30 second bins and fill the ``NaN``\n8889 |         values using the ``ffill`` method.\n8890 | \n8891 |         >>> series.resample(\"30s\").ffill()[0:5]\n8892 |         2000-01-01 00:00:00    0\n8893 |         2000-01-01 00:00:30    0\n8894 |         2000-01-01 00:01:00    1\n8895 |         2000-01-01 00:01:30    1\n8896 |         2000-01-01 00:02:00    2\n8897 |         Freq: 30s, dtype: int64\n8898 | \n8899 |         Upsample the series into 30 second bins and fill the\n8900 |         ``NaN`` values using the ``bfill`` method.\n8901 | \n8902 |         >>> series.resample(\"30s\").bfill()[0:5]\n8903 |         2000-01-01 00:00:00    0\n8904 |         2000-01-01 00:00:30    1\n8905 |         2000-01-01 00:01:00    1\n8906 |         2000-01-01 00:01:30    2\n8907 |         2000-01-01 00:02:00    2\n8908 |         Freq: 30s, dtype: int64\n8909 | \n8910 |         Pass a custom function via ``apply``\n8911 | \n8912 |         >>> def custom_resampler(arraylike):\n8913 |         ...     return np.sum(arraylike) + 5\n8914 |         >>> series.resample(\"3min\").apply(custom_resampler)\n8915 |         2000-01-01 00:00:00     8\n8916 |         2000-01-01 00:03:00    17\n8917 |         2000-01-01 00:06:00    26\n8918 |         Freq: 3min, dtype: int64\n8919 | \n8920 |         For DataFrame objects, the keyword `on` can be used to specify the\n8921 |         column instead of the index for resampling.\n8922 | \n8923 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame([10, 11, 9, 13, 14, 18, 17, 19], columns=[\"price\"])\n8924 |         >>> df[\"volume\"] = [50, 60, 40, 100, 50, 100, 40, 50]\n8925 |         >>> df[\"week_starting\"] = pd.date_range(\"01/01/2018\", periods=8, freq=\"W\")\n8926 |         >>> df\n8927 |            price  volume week_starting\n8928 |         0     10      50    2018-01-07\n8929 |         1     11      60    2018-01-14\n8930 |         2      9      40    2018-01-21\n8931 |         3     13     100    2018-01-28\n8932 |         4     14      50    2018-02-04\n8933 |         5     18     100    2018-02-11\n8934 |         6     17      40    2018-02-18\n8935 |         7     19      50    2018-02-25\n8936 |         >>> df.resample(\"ME\", on=\"week_starting\").mean()\n8937 |                        price  volume\n8938 |         week_starting\n8939 |         2018-01-31     10.75    62.5\n8940 |         2018-02-28     17.00    60.0\n8941 | \n8942 |         For a DataFrame with MultiIndex, the keyword `level` can be used to\n8943 |         specify on which level the resampling needs to take place.\n8944 | \n8945 |         >>> days = pd.date_range(\"1/1/2000\", periods=4, freq=\"D\")\n8946 |         >>> df2 = pd.DataFrame(\n8947 |         ...     [\n8948 |         ...         [10, 50],\n8949 |         ...         [11, 60],\n8950 |         ...         [9, 40],\n8951 |         ...         [13, 100],\n8952 |         ...         [14, 50],\n8953 |         ...         [18, 100],\n8954 |         ...         [17, 40],\n8955 |         ...         [19, 50],\n8956 |         ...     ],\n8957 |         ...     columns=[\"price\", \"volume\"],\n8958 |         ...     index=pd.MultiIndex.from_product([days, [\"morning\", \"afternoon\"]]),\n8959 |         ... )\n8960 |         >>> df2\n8961 |                               price  volume\n8962 |         2000-01-01 morning       10      50\n8963 |                    afternoon     11      60\n8964 |         2000-01-02 morning        9      40\n8965 |                    afternoon     13     100\n8966 |         2000-01-03 morning       14      50\n8967 |                    afternoon     18     100\n8968 |         2000-01-04 morning       17      40\n8969 |                    afternoon     19      50\n8970 |         >>> df2.resample(\"D\", level=0).sum()\n8971 |                     price  volume\n8972 |         2000-01-01     21     110\n8973 |         2000-01-02     22     140\n8974 |         2000-01-03     32     150\n8975 |         2000-01-04     36      90\n8976 | \n8977 |         If you want to adjust the start of the bins based on a fixed timestamp:\n8978 | \n8979 |         >>> start, end = \"2000-10-01 23:30:00\", \"2000-10-02 00:30:00\"\n8980 |         >>> rng = pd.date_range(start, end, freq=\"7min\")\n8981 |         >>> ts = pd.Series(np.arange(len(rng)) * 3, index=rng)\n8982 |         >>> ts\n8983 |         2000-10-01 23:30:00     0\n8984 |         2000-10-01 23:37:00     3\n8985 |         2000-10-01 23:44:00     6\n8986 |         2000-10-01 23:51:00     9\n8987 |         2000-10-01 23:58:00    12\n8988 |         2000-10-02 00:05:00    15\n8989 |         2000-10-02 00:12:00    18\n8990 |         2000-10-02 00:19:00    21\n8991 |         2000-10-02 00:26:00    24\n8992 |         Freq: 7min, dtype: int64\n8993 | \n8994 |         >>> ts.resample(\"17min\").sum()\n8995 |         2000-10-01 23:14:00     0\n8996 |         2000-10-01 23:31:00     9\n8997 |         2000-10-01 23:48:00    21\n8998 |         2000-10-02 00:05:00    54\n8999 |         2000-10-02 00:22:00    24\n9000 |         Freq: 17min, dtype: int64\n9001 | \n9002 |         >>> ts.resample(\"17min\", origin=\"epoch\").sum()\n9003 |         2000-10-01 23:18:00     0\n9004 |         2000-10-01 23:35:00    18\n9005 |         2000-10-01 23:52:00    27\n9006 |         2000-10-02 00:09:00    39\n9007 |         2000-10-02 00:26:00    24\n9008 |         Freq: 17min, dtype: int64\n9009 | \n9010 |         >>> ts.resample(\"17min\", origin=\"2000-01-01\").sum()\n9011 |         2000-10-01 23:24:00     3\n9012 |         2000-10-01 23:41:00    15\n9013 |         2000-10-01 23:58:00    45\n9014 |         2000-10-02 00:15:00    45\n9015 |         Freq: 17min, dtype: int64\n9016 | \n9017 |         If you want to adjust the start of the bins with an `offset` Timedelta, the two\n9018 |         following lines are equivalent:\n9019 | \n9020 |         >>> ts.resample(\"17min\", origin=\"start\").sum()\n9021 |         2000-10-01 23:30:00     9\n9022 |         2000-10-01 23:47:00    21\n9023 |         2000-10-02 00:04:00    54\n9024 |         2000-10-02 00:21:00    24\n9025 |         Freq: 17min, dtype: int64\n9026 | \n9027 |         >>> ts.resample(\"17min\", offset=\"23h30min\").sum()\n9028 |         2000-10-01 23:30:00     9\n9029 |         2000-10-01 23:47:00    21\n9030 |         2000-10-02 00:04:00    54\n9031 |         2000-10-02 00:21:00    24\n9032 |         Freq: 17min, dtype: int64\n9033 | \n9034 |         If you want to take the largest Timestamp as the end of the bins:\n9035 | \n9036 |         >>> ts.resample(\"17min\", origin=\"end\").sum()\n9037 |         2000-10-01 23:35:00     0\n9038 |         2000-10-01 23:52:00    18\n9039 |         2000-10-02 00:09:00    27\n9040 |         2000-10-02 00:26:00    63\n9041 |         Freq: 17min, dtype: int64\n9042 | \n9043 |         In contrast with the `start_day`, you can use `end_day` to take the ceiling\n9044 |         midnight of the largest Timestamp as the end of the bins and drop the bins\n9045 |         not containing data:\n9046 | \n9047 |         >>> ts.resample(\"17min\", origin=\"end_day\").sum()\n9048 |         2000-10-01 23:38:00     3\n9049 |         2000-10-01 23:55:00    15\n9050 |         2000-10-02 00:12:00    45\n9051 |         2000-10-02 00:29:00    45\n9052 |         Freq: 17min, dtype: int64\n9053 |         \"\"\"\n9054 |         from pandas.core.resample import get_resampler\n9055 | \n9056 |         if convention is not lib.no_default:\n9057 |             warnings.warn(\n9058 |                 f\"The 'convention' keyword in {type(self).__name__}.resample is \"\n9059 |                 \"deprecated and will be removed in a future version. \"\n9060 |                 \"Explicitly cast PeriodIndex to DatetimeIndex before resampling \"\n9061 |                 \"instead.\",\n9062 |                 FutureWarning,\n9063 |                 stacklevel=find_stack_level(),\n9064 |             )\n9065 |         else:\n9066 |             convention = \"start\"\n9067 | \n9068 |         return get_resampler(\n9069 |             cast(\"Series | DataFrame\", self),\n9070 |             freq=rule,\n9071 |             label=label,\n9072 |             clos